          %title Free quantum fields
          %h2#FreeQuantumFields Free quantum fields
          %p In this chapter we discuss the following topics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#WickAlgebraAbstract"} Wick algebra
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#AbstractTimeOrderedProduct"} Time-ordered product
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#OperatorProductAndNormalOrderedProduct"} Operator product notation
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#HadamardVacuumStatesOnWickAlgebras"} Hadamard vacuum state
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumBVDifferential"} Free quantum BV-differential
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#SchwingerDysonEquation"} Schwinger-Dyson equation
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            In the
            %a{:href => "#Quantization"} previous chapter
            we discussed
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of linear
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebra"} noncommutative algebra
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+spaces"} covariant phase spaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
            (as discussed in the chapter
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#GaugeFixing"} Gauge fixing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
          %p
            For this purpose we first need to find a sub-algebra of all observables which is large enough to contain all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
            (such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5En+interaction"} phi^n interaction
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InWickAlgebraphinInteraction"}
            below, and the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InWickAlgebraElectronPhotonInteraction"}
            below) but small enough for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            to meet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander%27s+criterion"} Hörmander's criterion
            for absence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
            (remark
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UltravioletDivergencesFromPaleyWiener"}
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PointwiseProductsOfFieldObservablesAdiabaticallySwitchedIsMicrocausal"}
            below): It is called the algebra of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+polynomial+observables"} microcausal polynomial observables
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MicrocausalObservable"}
            below).
          %div
            %p
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
              \:
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_dc72fc1ac31759c80d4dd998a660470e72d5ea5d_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mtext local
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mtext field
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mtext linear
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mtext microcausal
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mtext polynomial
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mtext general
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mtext regular
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\text{local}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    \\text{field}
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    \\text{linear}
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    \\text{microcausal}
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    \\text{polynomial}
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    \\text{general}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \&amp; \nearrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\text{regular}
                    }
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            still violates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander%27s+criterion"} Hörmander's criterion
            for absence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+polynomial+observables"} microcausal polynomial observables
            %a{:href => "#Quantization"} previous chapter
            that qantization freedom allows to shift this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+tensor"} Poisson tensor
            by a symmetric contribution. By prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SkewSymmetricPartOfHadmrdPropagatorIsCausalPropagatorForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            such a shift is provided by passage from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WaveFronSetsForKGPropagatorsOnMinkowski"}
            this reduces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
            and hence the UV-singularities “by half”.
          %p
            This way the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
            exists on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+polynomial+observables"} microcausal polynomial observables
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            algebra induced by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+algebra"} non-commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MoyalStarProductOnMicrocausal"}
            below). Its algebra structure may be expressed in terms of a commutative “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal-ordered+product"}> normal-ordered product
            ” (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NormalOrderedProductNotation"}
            below) and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+values"} vacuum expectation values
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
            in a canonically induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WickAlgebraCanonicalState"}
            below).
          %p
            The analogous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OnRegularPolynomialObservablesTimeOrderedProduct"}
            below) acts by first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+ordering"} causal ordering
            its arguments and then multiplying them with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra product
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CausalOrderingTimeOrderedProductOnRegular"}
            below) and hence is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OnRegularPolynomialObservablesTimeOrderedProduct"}
            below). This is the key structure in the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theory"} interacting field theory
            discussed in the next chapter
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InteractingQuantumFields"} Interacting quantum fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
            only, hence for averages of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
            that evaluate at distinct
